1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,411
The first photograph was not impressive. A wet bus

2
00:00:03,411 --> 00:00:06,822
stop. A crooked blue sign. His grandmother recognized it

3
00:00:06,822 --> 00:00:10,232
immediately. That is where we used to wait, she

4
00:00:10,232 --> 00:00:13,643
said.

5
00:00:14,063 --> 00:00:16,966
He decided to make her a small book of

6
00:00:16,966 --> 00:00:19,868
their old neighborhood. Not famous places. The bakery window,

7
00:00:19,868 --> 00:00:22,771
the footbridge, the bench where she always stopped to

8
00:00:22,771 --> 00:00:25,673
rest.

9
00:00:26,093 --> 00:00:29,747
He opened an account for inspiration. A photographer stood

10
00:00:29,747 --> 00:00:33,400
above a mountain. Another leaned from a helicopter over

11
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:37,053
a city he had never visited.

12
00:00:37,473 --> 00:00:40,320
He looked down at his own picture. The blue

13
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:43,168
sign suddenly seemed cheap. The rain looked gray instead

14
00:00:43,168 --> 00:00:46,015
of dramatic. Nothing in the photograph had changed except

15
00:00:46,015 --> 00:00:48,862
the comparison.

16
00:00:49,282 --> 00:00:52,623
On his next walk, he passed the bakery without

17
00:00:52,623 --> 00:00:55,964
taking a picture. He was waiting for better light

18
00:00:55,964 --> 00:00:59,305
now. Something cinematic. Something that would belong beside those

19
00:00:59,305 --> 00:01:02,646
other photographs.

20
00:01:03,066 --> 00:01:06,966
He listed what serious photographers seemed to have: better

21
00:01:06,966 --> 00:01:10,867
cameras, better clothes, more interesting mornings. At the bottom,

22
00:01:10,867 --> 00:01:14,768
he had forgotten the book.

23
00:01:15,188 --> 00:01:18,017
He studied their rooms as carefully as their pictures.

24
00:01:18,017 --> 00:01:20,846
Their work seemed to come from a life that

25
00:01:20,846 --> 00:01:23,675
had already been arranged correctly.

26
00:01:24,095 --> 00:01:26,856
His window faced a brick wall and a clothesline.

27
00:01:26,856 --> 00:01:29,616
He pulled the curtain across it. Then he spent

28
00:01:29,616 --> 00:01:32,376
the evening looking at someone else's view through a

29
00:01:32,376 --> 00:01:35,136
smaller rectangle.

30
00:01:35,556 --> 00:01:38,186
The camera stayed on his desk. He renamed his

31
00:01:38,186 --> 00:01:40,816
account and deleted the bus stop picture. It no

32
00:01:40,816 --> 00:01:43,445
longer matched the person he wanted to appear to

33
00:01:43,445 --> 00:01:46,075
be.

34
00:01:46,495 --> 00:01:50,245
On the train, every dark window became a mirror.

35
00:01:50,245 --> 00:01:53,995
He checked his reflection, then a stranger's photograph, then

36
00:01:53,995 --> 00:01:57,745
his reflection again. He was measuring two things that

37
00:01:57,745 --> 00:02:01,495
did not belong together.

38
00:02:01,915 --> 00:02:05,317
He had borrowed a scorecard without noticing. Their finished

39
00:02:05,317 --> 00:02:08,719
highlight against his entire ordinary day. Their chosen frame

40
00:02:08,719 --> 00:02:12,120
against everything he knew about himself. The comparison could

41
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:15,522
never be fair.

42
00:02:15,942 --> 00:02:18,922
Knowing the comparison was unfair did not immediately change

43
00:02:18,922 --> 00:02:21,902
how he felt. The camera gathered dust while his

44
00:02:21,902 --> 00:02:24,882
taste grew more demanding.

45
00:02:25,302 --> 00:02:28,097
His grandmother called on Sunday. Had he taken any

46
00:02:28,097 --> 00:02:30,892
new pictures? He said he was working on the

47
00:02:30,892 --> 00:02:33,687
idea. She asked whether the bakery still put bread

48
00:02:33,687 --> 00:02:36,482
in the window.

49
00:02:36,902 --> 00:02:40,112
He could not remember. He lived three streets away,

50
00:02:40,112 --> 00:02:43,322
but he had spent more time studying a mountain

51
00:02:43,322 --> 00:02:46,533
on another continent than noticing what happened outside his

52
00:02:46,533 --> 00:02:49,743
own door.

53
00:02:50,163 --> 00:02:53,535
The next morning, a handwritten notice appeared on the

54
00:02:53,535 --> 00:02:56,908
bakery glass. Closing next month. The owner was retiring.

55
00:02:56,908 --> 00:03:00,281
There would not be an unlimited number of ordinary

56
00:03:00,281 --> 00:03:03,654
mornings after all.

57
00:03:04,074 --> 00:03:07,673
He almost walked past. Then the owner stepped outside

58
00:03:07,673 --> 00:03:11,272
with a tray and asked whether he wanted anything.

59
00:03:11,272 --> 00:03:14,871
He stopped and bought one roll.

60
00:03:15,291 --> 00:03:18,100
He bought one roll and sat across the street.

61
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:20,910
Steam clouded the paper bag. The sign leaned slightly

62
00:03:20,910 --> 00:03:23,720
to the left. He knew that tilt. His grandmother

63
00:03:23,720 --> 00:03:26,529
would know it too.

64
00:03:26,949 --> 00:03:30,297
He wrote a different question in his notebook. Not,

65
00:03:30,297 --> 00:03:33,644
would a stranger admire this? Would someone who knows

66
00:03:33,644 --> 00:03:36,992
this place recognize what matters?

67
00:03:37,412 --> 00:03:41,043
This was the frame he could choose: his reason

68
00:03:41,043 --> 00:03:44,674
for making the book, one honest photograph, and the

69
00:03:44,674 --> 00:03:48,305
person he meant to share it with. A direction,

70
00:03:48,305 --> 00:03:51,936
not a popularity forecast.

71
00:03:52,356 --> 00:03:55,080
He raised the camera. A bus interrupted the first

72
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:57,805
shot. In the next, the owner laughed at a

73
00:03:57,805 --> 00:04:00,529
customer beyond the edge.

74
00:04:00,949 --> 00:04:03,962
The picture was uneven. He straightened it a little,

75
00:04:03,962 --> 00:04:06,975
then stopped. The laugh mattered more than the perfect

76
00:04:06,975 --> 00:04:09,988
angle. He wrote the date underneath and kept the

77
00:04:09,988 --> 00:04:13,001
photograph.

78
00:04:13,421 --> 00:04:16,396
When he opened the reference account that evening, the

79
00:04:16,396 --> 00:04:19,371
mountain was still beautiful. He saved it without turning

80
00:04:19,371 --> 00:04:22,346
it into a verdict. Admiration did not have to

81
00:04:22,346 --> 00:04:25,321
become an instruction to disappear.

82
00:04:25,741 --> 00:04:28,600
Over the next week, he returned to the footbridge.

83
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:31,459
The railings had been repainted. He took a close

84
00:04:31,459 --> 00:04:34,318
photograph of a small patch where the old blue

85
00:04:34,318 --> 00:04:37,177
still showed through.

86
00:04:37,597 --> 00:04:40,464
At the bench, he waited until the rain stopped.

87
00:04:40,464 --> 00:04:43,332
Not for a dramatic sky. He wanted the wet

88
00:04:43,332 --> 00:04:46,200
wood and the empty space beside it, the place

89
00:04:46,200 --> 00:04:49,067
she usually put her bag.

90
00:04:49,487 --> 00:04:52,512
He photographed the bus stop again. This time, he

91
00:04:52,512 --> 00:04:55,536
included the crooked sign. He could imagine his grandmother

92
00:04:55,536 --> 00:04:58,560
pointing at it before she read a single word

93
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:01,585
of the caption.

94
00:05:02,005 --> 00:05:05,472
Some pictures did not work. One was blurred. Another

95
00:05:05,472 --> 00:05:08,940
said nothing. He left them out. His own purpose

96
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:12,407
still needed choices and care.

97
00:05:12,827 --> 00:05:16,154
He asked a friend to look through the pages.

98
00:05:16,154 --> 00:05:19,480
She suggested moving the bakery photograph earlier. He listened.

99
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:22,806
A personal standard could still make room for useful

100
00:05:22,806 --> 00:05:26,132
eyes besides his own.

101
00:05:26,552 --> 00:05:29,777
One night, he slipped back into comparing. A younger

102
00:05:29,777 --> 00:05:33,002
photographer had won an award. He stared until his

103
00:05:33,002 --> 00:05:36,226
small book felt ridiculous. Then he remembered who was

104
00:05:36,226 --> 00:05:39,451
waiting for it.

105
00:05:39,871 --> 00:05:42,820
He closed the account and printed the next page.

106
00:05:42,820 --> 00:05:45,769
It was an ordinary choice, and he would need

107
00:05:45,769 --> 00:05:48,718
to make it again. One evening of clarity had

108
00:05:48,718 --> 00:05:51,667
not ended the habit.

109
00:05:52,087 --> 00:05:55,496
On the last page, he wrote: Some things changed.

110
00:05:55,496 --> 00:05:58,906
Some things stayed. I thought you might like to

111
00:05:58,906 --> 00:06:02,315
see. He left that sentence alone.

112
00:06:02,735 --> 00:06:05,853
He mailed the book in a plain envelope. No

113
00:06:05,853 --> 00:06:08,970
public announcement. No promise that it would change anyone's

114
00:06:08,970 --> 00:06:12,087
life. Just twelve photographs that had finally reached the

115
00:06:12,087 --> 00:06:15,204
person they were for.

116
00:06:15,624 --> 00:06:18,233
His grandmother called and began with the crooked sign.

117
00:06:18,233 --> 00:06:20,841
Then she asked about the bakery owner. They talked

118
00:06:20,841 --> 00:06:23,449
for an hour.

119
00:06:23,869 --> 00:06:26,894
Later, he opened his curtain. The brick wall was

120
00:06:26,894 --> 00:06:29,918
still there. So was the clothesline. A yellow shirt

121
00:06:29,918 --> 00:06:32,943
moved in the wind. For the first time in

122
00:06:32,943 --> 00:06:35,967
weeks, he reached for the camera.

123
00:06:36,387 --> 00:06:39,002
Someone else's life can be beautiful without making yours

124
00:06:39,002 --> 00:06:41,617
a mistake. Before you change everything to fit their

125
00:06:41,617 --> 00:06:44,233
frame, ask what you wanted to see through your

126
00:06:44,233 --> 00:06:46,848
own.
